    "body": "Yes, I agree it's semantically incorrect use case. Sorry, I couldn't come up with a better example, my example catered to demostrating what I was trying to talk about.\r\n\r\nNow Let me put the issue another way:\r\n\r\nConsider two `div`s are specified with `display: inline`, they are next to each other, and they both contain content-generated text which probably is bidi-directional.\r\n\r\nNow since the spec says `div` conveys no semantic meaning, then the default unicode-bidi value in the recommended stylesheet can cause inconsistent results with the semantics.\r\n\r\nOf course, although a deisnger can always explicitly set `unicode-bidi` to `normal` when `display` is set to `inline`, so that the rendering and semantics are always guaranteed to be consistent, but I just suspect vast majority of designers in reality failed to do things correctly(while `div` used as `inline` is common usage). So I just suggest we add statements that implicitly add an additional level of text content. This matches vast majority of `inline div` usages in real world.",
