    "created_at": "2018-08-15T13:14:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-08-15T13:14:52Z",
    "body": "A couple of points i'd like to make:\r\n1. we (W3C internationalization WG, and the HTML & CSS specs) strongly recommend that bidi behaviour be managed in HTML using markup, and not CSS properties – although, of course, the browser's default implementation of the behaviour of HTML's dedicated bidi markup is defined in terms of CSS in the HTML spec. In general, it should be possible to not apply any CSS and still see your content in a readable fashion. You cannot, however, display bidi text in a readable fashion if the bidi behaviour is specified in the content author's CSS styling and the CSS is removed. For this reason, i tend to view bidi markup as 'pseudo-semantic'.   Authors should use the `dir` attribute or other bidi markup to establish the base direction.\r\n2. I suspect that there's an incorrect assumption in this thread.  While it's true that `unicode-bidi:isolate` is a relatively recent addition to CSS, the default should really have always been to  isolate runs of text in blocks, and even in inline text where the base direction changes. Otherwise there are (and prior to the addition of `isolate` there were) many problems to make bidi text work correctly. (The Unicode Standard also encourages the use of isolating format characters, rather than the non-isolating ones.) So div is not applying any special behaviour by defaulting to `unicode-bidi:isolate` – that is the expected default.\r\n3. Even when making divs or list items into inline tabs, say, you normally want to preserve the isolation, otherwise you are likely to experience interference across phrase boundaries. (See the section [Adjacent ranges often need to be isolated](https://www.w3.org/International/articles/inline-bidi-markup/uba-basics#isolation) in my primer for bidi for a simple illustration of interference effects when discrete items are arranged sequentially inline.)\r\n\r\nI hope that helps a little.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
