    "updated_at": "2018-08-15T15:35:12Z",
    "body": "`div` is the \"block\" meaningless element; `span` is the \"inline\" meaningless element. Yes, that's a tiny bit of meaning assigned to each of them, but it's too trivial to call \"semantics\" - a screen reader, for example, can't derive anything from that fact.  The two of them exist because it's convenient to have meaningless elements, and block vs inline is a very important styling distinction that's useful to express right away, in the element name, rather than needing to apply extra CSS.  And then `div` gets bidi-isolated by default, because that's almost certainly the correct thing to do for things that are probably going to be blocks (while it's not quite a sure thing for `span`, so it's not applied there).\r\n\r\nSo, the spec is still correct in its statements.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
